<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amadeus VI, Count of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1334–<a href="/years/1383">1383</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Green Count, Amadeus VI of Savoy · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amadeus VI, known as the Green Count from the colour he wore at tournaments, ruled Savoy from 1343 to 1383. He expanded his lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> and founded the Order of the Collar. In 1366 he led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> to aid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and he died of plague on an Italian campaign in 1383.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amadeus-vi-count-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Amadeus VI, Count of Savoy" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea di Bonaiuto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Am%C3%A9d%C3%A9e_VI_de_Savoie,_le_Comte_Vert.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amadeus VI, eldest son of Aymon, Count of Savoy and Yolande Palaeologina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, inherited the county in 1343 at the age of nine. His father's will named two cousins, Amadeus III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and Louis II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>, as co-regents; neither could make a significant decision without the other, and both were subject to review by a council of nobles. His cousin Joan of Savoy renewed a claim to the county, which Savoy's Semi-Salic tradition had already denied, and in 1345 Philip, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, to whom she had left it, yielded the claim for 5000 livres annually.</p><p>He was taught to fight and ride and read classical works such as De Re Militari. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> ravaged Savoy from 1348 to 1351, halving the population in some villages. Peasants blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> for poisoning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> a mob broke into the castle where Jews had been locked for protection and killed several. Eleven more were executed and the rest fined 160 florins a month for six years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1352 Amadeus put down a rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>, defeating the rebels on 3 November, tearing down part of the wall of Sion and fining its citizens. He took his nickname, the Green Count, in 1353, when he appeared at tournaments in green plumes and a green <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> tabard, with eleven knights in green each led by a lady in green.</p><p>Fighting against Hugh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and the Dauphiné continued until April 1354. By the treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1355 he gave up territory beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhone</a> and Guiers in return for recognition as sovereign of Faucigny and Gex and suzerain over the Counts of Geneva. That year he married Bonne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>.</p><p>In 1366 he led a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> of 15 ships and 1,700 men against the Ottoman Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a>, to help his cousin John V Palaiologos, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-savoy">Anna of Savoy</a>. With Francesco I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> he drove the Turks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, a gain lost a few years later. John V was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, so Amadeus took Mesembria and Sozopolis, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> and forced Tsar Ivan Alexander to release him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1377 Amadeus ordered the region's first public <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mechanical-clock">mechanical clock</a> and established a system of state-supported poor relief, one of the first of its kind in the late medieval world. In 1381 he mediated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> the peace ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. In 1382 he joined Louis I, Duke of Anjou, on an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and shared in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. He died near Castropignano in 1383 and was buried at Hautecombe Abbey. His son Amadeus VII succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amadeus VI of Savoy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Amadeus VI</b>, the Green Count, led a crusade in 1366, and Amadeus VII acquired Nice in 1388.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-savoy">County of Savoy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Santo Stefano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.57,5.92&amp;year=1365" aria-label="Chambéry on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Amadeus VI, Count of Savoy"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5354 6206h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5354 6206h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.57,5.92&amp;year=1365">What was here in 1365? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
